\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late;Peso Down On Monetary Easing \b0\par\par\uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2003\par 12:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag late Thursday, caught between strong stock market performance and concern that central banks might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended lower after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively eased monetary policy in a move that was widely expected. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also cut its overnight money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate Thursday, its sixth such reduction so far this year. The Indonesian rupiah closed lower after a choppy session. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was little changed. The Singapore dollar, Thai baht and New Taiwan dollar were firmer. \par \par \uc2  The tone of the broad market during the day was biased toward a softer U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the flow of foreign funds into regional equity markets, said a trader at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/Asians are on a soft-ish bias. Basically, (Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are trading on a strong stock market performance as capital flows come into the region," he said. \par \par \uc2  There was also a lot of play on euro crosses as market players sold the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a widely anticipated rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later Thursday, he added. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America, in a research note, said a combination of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and easing bias in interest rate policy globally is allowing Asian central banks room to maneuver monetary policy to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  "Low interest rates and abundant liquidity is giving support to regional stock markets - in turn aiding further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. Regional central banks tread the fine line of allowing appreciation as a result of greater foreign capital inflows, and resisting any damage to their export competitiveness," Bank of America said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 53.330 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.270 Wednesday, but off the intraday high of PHP53.415. \par \par \uc2  Traders said many banks took a long dollar position because of a narrowing of the differential between U.S. and local interest rates from the easing of local monetary policy, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effected monetary easing by reinstating the tiered interest rate system on its overnight borrowing rates. The system, which pays lower rates for funds in excess of PHP5 billion, was scrapped in March to quell speculative demand for the dollar, which at that time was trading at PHP55. \par \par \uc2  "Some banks covered their short positions on the dollar because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move," said a trader with a local bank. Some banks also bought dollars for corporate clients. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the peso's fall was triggered by the weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and not by the return of the tiered system. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, a combination of profit taking and dollar demand from local importers pulled the rupiah lower after a volatile session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed local trading at 8,225 rupiah, up from IDR8,165 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market players took profit on the rupiah after it earlier rose to a 34-month intraday high of 8,140 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Thursday, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its overnight money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to 10.25% from 10.50%. \par \par \uc2  Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah told Parliament that domestic interest rates will fall further amid a benign inflation outlook. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won finished almost unchanged in quiet pre-holiday trade. Local financial markets will be shut Friday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,200.3 won, from KRW1,200.6 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The market is wary of testing the dollar below KRW1,200 after banks bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under that level Wednesday. Many suspect the buying was done on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market was restrained on the downside by the BOK (Bank of Korea) and on the upside by dollar selling by exporters and for stock settlements," said a dealer. "Traders are losing interest in trading." \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought Korean shares for a seventh session, ending as net buyers of KRW187.4 billion of shares Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also finished higher on the back of a strong stock market, as well as on the yen's gains versus the U.S. unit, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.665, down from NT$34.687 Tuesday, marking its strongest close since May 21. Local markets were closed for a holiday Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Pent-up demand for U.S. dollars after the holiday, however, helped pull the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off its session lows, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was modestly stronger late in Asia, although it came off highs as the yen retreated slightly against the U.S. dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7233, compared with S$1.7248 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Aside from brief spurts of activity, trade in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remained quiet. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone's lost interest to trade. It's been very quiet for the past two days, aside from some cross-related trade," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody's watching the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) today for direction," he added. \par \par \uc2  As against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted around 41.60 late in Asia, down from THB41.68 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030605dz65000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
