\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late On Yen; New Dlr Buying Interest \b0\par\par\uc2 882 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 11:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell Thursday as the dollar strengthened against the yen, which briefly weakened to a near-four week low as market players unwound short-dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing new interest in buying dollars," against global majors, and a similar trend was also apparent against regional units, a trader at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  But whether that pattern can be sustained is moot, analysts and traders said. Although economies in the region are seeing a slowdown in growth, and the trend appears to be increasingly for further monetary easing, the U.S. economic outlook remains cloudy. \par \par \uc2  "Just as the upside (for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is contained by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the downside is contained," by relatively stronger fundamentals in Asia, said Vincent Low, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond strategy for Asia at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "My sense of it is, people still want to consider reloading longs on some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah, for instance, have been attracting strong buying interest in recent days; the former supported by robust domestic demand, and the latter by its high yields. \par \par \uc2  Their losses have hence been limited even as the dollar strengthens. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries like Singapore, which is heavily dependent on external demand, are more vulnerable to any dollar correction, Low said. \par \par \uc2  As against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7362 late in the local session, up from S$1.7274 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at 41.86 versus the Thai baht late in Asia, from THB41.69 previously. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted around 8,350 against the Indonesian rupiah, up from IDR8,220 late Wednesday, due to month-end demand from local companies to repay offshore debt ahead of a long weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Thursday was the last day for companies to buy dollars as local markets will be closed Friday for a public holiday \par \par \uc2  Market players also trimmed short-dollar positions following the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rebound against major rivals and other Asian units. \par \par \uc2  A cut in Bank Indonesia's policy rate failed to move the market as it was widely expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the overnight money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by 25 basis points to 10.50% amid easing inflation. This is the second rate cut this month and the fifth this year. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, the U.S. unit closed at 34.772 against the New Taiwan dollar, up from NT$34.730 Wednesday, tracking its rise against the yen and the South Korean won, as well as other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan and South Korea are two of Taiwan's top export competitors. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around Y118.87, up from Y118.65 overnight in New York, and Y117.10 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was weighed down by the weak yen, and also Minister of Finance and Economy Kim Jin Pyo's remarks that the government will conduct smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb speculation. \par \par \uc2  Exporters selling dollars to balance their books at the end of the month saved the won from steeper losses, dealers said. The dollar finished at 1,207.0 won, up from KRW1,200.4 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Recently the government strongly showed its intention to support the market," said Gin Lee, head of fixed income at Credit Agricole Indosuez. "The Korean economy is getting worse and worse. The Korean won cannot be stronger considering the economy." \par \par \uc2  South Korea's weak industrial output data for April, released earlier Thursday, paint a grim picture of the economy with all key components pointing to a further economic slowdown in coming months as capital investment shrinks and consumption cools further. \par \par \uc2  April industrial production rose 1.8% on year, slowing from a 5.0% rise in March. It was the weakest growth since September 2002, and was worse than the average 3.4% rise predicted in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed at a two-month low on the back of the yen-led decline in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thinning remittance inflows from Filipinos working abroad, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at its intraday high of 53.350 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, its highest since April 1, and up from PHP53.100 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said first-quarter gross domestic product growth data, released earlier Thursday, didn't have an impact on the market as players focused on the surging dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Philippine GDP rose 4.5% on year in the January-March period, at the higher end of the government 4%-4.5% projection, and accelerating from 3.8% in the year-earlier quarter. But it was lower than the 5.8% rise in the last quarter of 2002. \par \par \uc2  A local bank trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have bought dollars to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves ahead of payment of maturing debt in October. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't immediately be reached for comment. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was also some corporate demand for dollars to cover month-end requirements. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Kevin Lim \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030529dz5t000my\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
