\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Up At 32-Mo High On Ratings Upgrade \b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2003\par 12:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended at a 32-month high against the dollar Monday as Standard and Poor's Ratings Service upgraded its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on Indonesia, further bolstering already bullish sentiment for the country, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  S&P raised the rating to B Minus from CCC Plus, reflecting the diminished likelihood that government will seek additional debt restructuring, a situation made possible by its improving fiscal and external positions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,430 rupiah, down from Friday's close at IDR8,555. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the rupiah turned positive in April as the much-feared violent protests against the Iraqi war in the world's most populous Muslim country didn't happen. Foreign investors started to park their funds in Indonesia's capital markets as the war in Iraq approached its end last month, slowly driving the rupiah higher from levels around IDR9,000. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Indonesian stocks have risen around 23% during the same period as foreign investors snapped up undervalued blue-chip stocks. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia's deputy governor Miranda Gultom said Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't concerned about the dollar's fall to below IDR8,500 as exporters can still be competitive at that level. She also said the benchmark one-month rate of Bank Indonesia's Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes may fall to 10.5% by the end of the year from 10.91% currently if "conditions keep improving." \par \par \uc2  "Her comment is an endorsement for the market's intention to drive the dollar to near IDR8,000," said a dealer with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers were still concerned the rupiah's sharp rise could trigger Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's appreciation has helped reduce inflation to 7.54% on year in April, and consumer prices are likely to continue falling in the coming months, which will pave the way for further interest rate cuts. Cutting rates is expected to spur economic growth as well as help reduce the costs of bailing out the country's banking system. \par \par \uc2  President Megawati Sukarnoputri's rise to power in July 2001 has improved political stability in the country, and that has over time helped to improve investor sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Also, the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency's plan to sell a 51% stake in nationalized PT Bank Danamon Indonesia to Singapore's Temasek Holdings and Deutsche Bank, following its sale of PT Bank Central Asia and PT Bank Niaga last year, showed that the government is serious about implementing economic reform programs. \par \par \uc2  But analysts warn that the rupiah's strength is mostly underpinned by short-term capital inflows, which could easily exit the country. \par \par \uc2  Long-term foreign direct investment, which is the true measure of foreign investors' confidence in the country, remains scanty as the government has been slow in reforming its weak and corrupt legal system. \par \par \uc2  Still, dealers said there's still a lot of upward momentum in the rupiah, and the dollar Tuesday is expected to head lower toward IDR8,300. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Ryan Woo \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030512dz5c000ny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
