\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Gains cut on war moves, virus. \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2003\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, April 8 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trimmed\par offshore gains on Tuesday morning after the U.S. military\par bombed a target where it believed the Iraqi leader may have\par been and as mounting casualties from the SARS virus unsettled\par traders.\par     The Singapore dollar       , Thai baht        and Taiwan\par dollar  rose initially along with the Japanese yen\par   as euphoric U.S. dollar buying on expectations of a\par quick end to the Iraq war ran out of steam.\par     But news that the U.S. military had bombed a target in\par Baghdad where Iraqi President Saddam Hussein may have been\par spurred another U.S. dollar rally. Still, most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par remained a touch stronger than their levels on Monday\par afternoon.\par     "In the near-term, the market will continue to trade on how\par the war is shaping up in Iraq," DBS Bank economist Lee Boon\par Keng said in a commentary.\par     "And we expect the victory dance to stay for a little\par longer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Hence, the dollar will stay relatively well-bid, and\par bonds will continue to face downside pressure."\par     Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were also grappling with anxiety over the\par Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) virus, which claimed\par its 100th global victim and has infected over 2,600.\par     SINGAPORE GROWTH WORRIES\par     In Singapore, where eight people have died of the disease,\par markets worried about the prime minister's remark on Monday\par that growth forecasts would be revised down.\par     The Sing dollar weakened to 1.7810/20 to the U.S. dollar\par after a brief spike up to the firmer side of 1.78. On Monday,\par it hit five-month lows near 1.7850.\par     Speculation was rife over whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Singapore would tolerate a weaker Singapore dollar, outside the\par undisclosed trade-weighted band it is managed in.\par     The Thai baht        fell to a 3-=   month low of 43.24 in\par offshore deals, rallied and then weakened again as local\par markets resumed trade after a holiday.\par     One-year forwards on the Hong Kong dollar peg         were\par slightly higher around 210 basis points. The forwards rose\par markedly last week on market talk SARS could put pressure on\par Hong Kong's economy, its reserves and hence the HK dollar peg.\par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may not react very positively to the end\par of the war because there still are a few risk factors such as\par North Korea, SARS that loom over this region," UBS Warburg's\par strategist Bhanu Baweja said.\par     PESO, RUPIAH INDIFFERENT\par     The Philippine peso        maintained a steady 53.15-53.20\par range in morning deals, indifferent not just to the regional\par swings but also to news that the government had overshot its\par first quarter budget deficit target by 10 percent.\par     The Indonesian rupiah        was surprisingly strong even\par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut rates. Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut its\par overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate by 25 basis points to 11.25 percent\par on Monday.\par     The Korean won                   and Taiwan dollar mimicked\par the early gains in the yen, but markets were suspicious of the\par former's gains amid expectations the North Korean risk will\par escalate ahead of a U.N. Security Council debate on the\par country's suspected nuclear arms programme this week.\par  \par     The popular yen-won cross            too was steady around\par 10.47, having come off 10.68 last week.\par     "We do consider dollar-won to still be a buy on dips given\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that foreign outflows, a declining trade balance and the North\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Korea risk are still potent factors for the market," Baweja's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par note said.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0235 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.93       120.73         +0.67\par  Sing dlr      1.7805       1.7821         +0.09\par  Taiwan dlr    34.822       34.812         -0.03\par  Korean won   1256.30      1258.30         +0.16\par  Baht           43.10        43.17         +0.16\par  Peso           53.01        53.25         +0.45\par  Rupiah       8873.00      8892.00         +0.21\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.93       118.74         -0.99\par  Sing dlr      1.7805       1.7341         -2.61\par  Taiwan dlr    34.822       34.753         -0.20\par  Korean won   1256.30      1185.80         -5.61\par  Baht           43.10        43.08         -0.05\par  Peso           53.01        53.38         +0.70\par  Rupiah       8873.00      8940.00         +0.76.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030408dz480060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
