\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late;Choppy Trade On War News \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 933 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2003\par 10:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly firmer late Tuesday in a choppy session marked by reports on developments in the war in Iraq and speculation about the fate of Iraqi President Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  started the day on a stronger footing following overnight profit-taking on the dollar following recent gains. \par \par \uc2  But some weakness began to emerge following a wave of dollar buying in the Asian morning on talk that Saddam may have been killed in a bomb strike on a building in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The speculation was fueled by reports that U.S. forces bombed a "leadership target" in Baghdad. \par \par \uc2  The target was believed to be one or more of the top government or military leaders in Saddam Hussein's regime, according to military officials. \par \par \uc2  The attack, and the ensuring rumors about Saddam's fate, pushed the dollar briefly to an intraday high around Y120.16, up from Y119.86 overnight in New York. \par \par \uc2  "There was quite a lot of dollar buying as the market reacted to the news," a trader at a European bank said, noting that this caused "the weakening trend of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reassert itself." \par \par \uc2  This was compounded by concerns over the economic impact of the deadly severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS, outbreak, he said. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar buying wasn't sustained and as of 0830 GMT, the dollar had dropped back to around Y119.65. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar came into the Asian session a little bit 'positive news weary'. That set the tone for the Asian session. Within that, there was a burst of dollar buying but it wasn't really sustained," said David Simmonds, senior emerging markets strategist for Asia at Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was off session highs as concerns about the economic fallout of the SARS outbreak continued to plague the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of local trading, the dollar was quoted at S$1.7810, down from S$1.7849 late in Asia Monday, but off the intraday trough of S$1.7780. \par \par \uc2  Singapore has been one of the countries most affected by SARS. Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong said over the weekend that the government's forecast for a 2%-5% economic growth for 2003 would have to be revised downward as the viral outbreak takes its toll on the local travel and hospitality sectors. \par \par \uc2  The city state's advance gross domestic product estimates for the first quarter will be released Thursday, and market watchers say the government may also come up with a more up-to-date forecast for the full year. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed at a five-month high after the long weekend, bolstered by expectations of a quick conclusion to the war in Iraq, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some remittance inflows amid a lack of corporate demand for dollars also strengthened the peso, some traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at its intraday trough of 52.880 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System - its lowest close since Nov. 7, 2002 - and down from PHP53.250 Friday. Local financial markets were closed for a public holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone was worried about the Iraq crisis. The nearing conclusion of this crisis is causing the peso to reverse its downtrend," said Jose Emmanuel Hilado, a member of the Open Market Committee of the Bankers Association of the Philippines, which owns and operates the Philippine Dealing System. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won firmed in tandem with the yen with the dollar closing at KRW1,253.5, down from KRW1,257.6 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit has been sandwiched between KRW1,250 and KRW1,260 for more than a week as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped its upside while offshore buying provided support. \par \par \uc2  Offshore players are poised to buy dollars on dips on expectations that with a quick end to the war in Iraq, the U.S. would next focus its attention on the nuclear standoff with the communist North, a move that would undermine the won. \par \par \uc2  "Because of North Korea risk, offshore players are all looking to buy (dollars) on dips," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also took its cue from the yen, with the U.S. unit ending at NT$34.818, down from NT$34.844 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht shrugged off early losses after the long weekend and was quoted around 43.05 to the dollar late in Asia, from THB43.09 Friday. Local financial markets were shut for a holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spent much of the Asian session locked between THB43.12 and THB43.15, an offshore dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, Thailand's Finance Ministry expects the economic fallout from the SARS outbreak to trim the country's gross domestic product growth by 0.3-0.5 percentage point this year, a source at the ministry's Fiscal Policy Office said earlier Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The ministry's most up-to-date forecast is for the economy to expand 5.1% in 2003. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was also firmer with the dollar quoted around IDR8,877 late in Asia, from IDR8,885 Monday. \par \par \uc2  There was some suspicion earlier that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to accelerate the dollar's slide. \par \par \uc2  "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely wants to see what support level for the pair the market wants," a dealer said, but noted there was limited downside as the dollar is near the psychologically important IDR8,850 level. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Barry Deely and Kevin Lim \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030408dz48000kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
