\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late As Dlr Trims Gains Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled out of early troughs and were mixed late Monday as the dollar trimmed gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, Singapore dollar and Philippine peso were weaker, the Indonesian rupiah was firmer, and the New Taiwan dollar and Thai baht were little changed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar came off a three-month high of Y121.88 reached in New York Friday on signs the war in Iraq may not be progressing as smoothly as expected. \par \par \uc2  Going into the second day of the war Friday, market players had been expecting "that the war could finish fast. That's why they bought dollars," a trader at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  But following weekend reports of fierce Iraqi resistance and the capture of some U.S. troops, "people are feeling a little bit different now," though they still expect the U.S. to prevail, the trader added. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar gave up some of Friday's sharp gains against the yen, market players took the opportunity to also book profits from its rise against Asian units. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg said in a research note that developments on the war front over the weekend, and signs that Saddam Hussein is alive "have all worked to erode the war buoyancy of markets that was so much in evidence on Friday. \par \par \uc2  There was speculation that Saddam was killed during the initial strikes on Iraq when the U.S. launched military action during the Asian morning Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Most importantly, there is no sign as yet that pro-Saddam forces will simply fold, or that Iraq's general population are treating the allied forces as liberators, both factors seen as vital ingredients for a quick and easy war," UBS Warburg said. \par \par \uc2  Saddam addressed his nation late Monday in Asia, vowing that "victory will be ours soon," \par \par \uc2  He hailed Iraqi "bravery" and said Iraqi fighters were "causing the enemy to suffer and to lose every day." \par \par \uc2  "As time goes by, they will lose more and they will not be able to escape lightly from their predicament," he said. "We will make it as painful as we can." \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was slightly lower as concerns over a protracted war in Iraq had a greater negative impact on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than on the dollar, which ended at 1,251.0 won, up from KRW1,247.4 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit briefly spiked to a high of KRW1,255.2 in the morning session before falling back to touch a low of KRW1,248.0 on selling by offshore players and local exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar buying by oil refineries helped support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the afternoon, though trading came to a near standstill ahead of the Iraqi President's speech. \par \par \uc2  "Trading was almost on hold toward the end, as all wanted to get a clue from Saddam Hussein's speech," said a dealer at Kookmin Bank. \par \par \uc2  As against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7678 near the end of local trading hours, off a session high of S$1.7710, but still modestly up from S$1.7655 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 54.500 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.450 Friday, after trading between PHP54.410 and PHP54.510. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was nearly flat as U.S. dollar selling by exporters offset purchases by oil importers and day traders, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit closed at NT$34.788 after trading between NT$34.720 and NT$34.850. It ended Friday at NT$34.791. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought U.S. dollars to make sure the local dollar didn't strengthen too much, a dealer at a foreign bank in Taiwan said. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, dealers also suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the rupiah, which earlier fell as low as 8,995 to the dollar following weekend warnings by the U.S. and U.K. that terrorists may launch an attack on their citizens in the country. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at IDR8,955, down from IDR8,965 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank Indonesia was heard offering the dollar when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved close to the psychologically important IDR9,000 level. \par \par \uc2  The government is aiming for an average dollar exchange rate of IDR9,000 this year. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Pridiyathorn Devakula Monday reiterated that he was comfortable with the current baht movements, though he wasn't complacent about it. \par \par \uc2  "Fluctuations of half a percent against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't a concern," Pridiyathorn told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around THB42.95, off an early high of THB43.05. The dollar was buying THB42.98 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Barry Deely \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030324dz3o000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
