\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Lower After Anti-US Protests \b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended slightly lower versus the dollar Monday, hurt by weekend protests against the U.S. military buildup in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to IDR8,890 in the morning but ended at IDR8,885, still higher than IDR8,880 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Thousands protested in Indonesia over the weekend over a possible U.S.-led invasion of Iraq. Anti-U.S. feeling is often expressed in Indonesia, the world's most populous Muslim nation. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar bias, traders are unwilling to take large positions in the market. With trade very thin due to lack of investor interest in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants are fearful of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would cut any dollar gains. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars in recent weeks to stop the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving beyond IDR8,900. That level should form a stout resistance for the dollar Tuesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  -By Jakarta Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 6221 3983 1277; djn.jakarta@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030310dz3a000lz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
