\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Rupiah Ends Up Amid Global Anti-War Protests \b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2003\par 10:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended higher Monday as players cut long-dollar positions amid growing public opposition worldwide to a war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 8,900 rupiah, down from its close Friday at IDR8,930. \par \par \uc2  Investors bought dollars last week amid fears a war on Iraq will spark anti-U.S. demonstrations and possibly unrest in Indonesia, hurting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But players began unwinding long-dollar positions Monday as some parties felt war may be avoided due to growing public opposition in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also unable to sustain its gains in thin trade with the overhanging threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars last week to temper the dollar's rise but wasn't active Monday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR8,900 and IDR9,000 this week. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Wright, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; Tom.Wrightdowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030217dz2h000kx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
