\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; But Dlr Still Vulnerable \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2003\par 10:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar opened the new year on a firm note against most of its Asian counterparts, but longer-term prospects for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained clouded by a possible war in Iraq, and concerns about the health of U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Helped by its relatively strong performance against the yen, the dollar notched gains against all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Much of the dollar's support came from players covering their short positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its recent steep declines in the region, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been "looking oversold," said Jimmy Koh, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at United Overseas Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "There was some pullback in dollar selling, but the fundamentals are still there for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  going forward," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is likely to remain under pressure until at least the end of January when United Nations weapons inspectors are scheduled to deliver their report on Iraq's compliance with U.N. resolutions regarding disarmament, according to Koh. \par \par \uc2  Market participants will also be looking to upcoming economic data to see whether recent signs of weakness in the U.S. economy are temporary, or spell more trouble ahead, he said. \par \par \uc2  And even if the dollar manages to clear those obstacles, it will still face potentially crippling effects of the mammoth U.S. current account deficit, which is approaching 5% of gross domestic product, Koh added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to grind its way back above the Y119 level for the first time in three sessions, hitting an intraday high of Y119.41. More verbal threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's gains helped keep a floor under the dollar. \par \par \uc2  By the end of Asian trading the dollar had slipped back to Y119.05, still above Y118.70 in New York late Tuesday. Major global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were closed Wednesday for the New Year holiday. \par \par \uc2  Importers were doing most of the dollar buying against the South Korean won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,192.9l, above Tuesday's close at KRW1,186.5. \par \par \uc2  Dealing rooms were sparsely staffed after the New Year holiday and thin trading conditions exaggerated the won's moves. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore announced that it would maintain its neutral policy stance toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS also disclosed that the Singapore dollar had been trading for some time in the stronger half of the MAS' undisclosed target band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was taken to mean the defacto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not tolerate further gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7432, up from S$1.7378 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Also, some dealers took advantage of the recent broad U.S. dollar weakness to reestablish long U.S. dollar positions, with the aim of taking profit later. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, commercial demand from importers and interbank short-covering supported the U.S. dollar against the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.791, near its intraday high of NT$34.792, and up from NT$34.753 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often tracks the yen, due to the close trade ties between Taiwan and Japan, and because Taiwan and Japan compete in several export categories. \par \par \uc2  Hedging by Philippine companies and thinning remittance inflows from Filipinos working overseas weighed on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP53.470 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.300 Friday, the last time the peso was traded. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the demand for dollars rose as oil companies and other importers braced for a a weaker peso going forward. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slipped in part due to a series of bomb attacks and an ambush that killed 30 people and injured dozens in the southern Philippines in the last two weeks. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah ended higher against the dollar in volatile trading Thursday thanks to alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  well bid, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,915 rupiah, down from IDR8,950 late Tuesday in thin offshore trade. Local financial markets were closed Tuesday in advance of New Year's Day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market after the dollar rose to an intraday high of IDR8,995 on the back of demand from local companies. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar was quoted at THB43.110 in late Asian trading, down from THB43.130 late Tuesday. Financial markets in Bangkok were closed Tuesday, but the baht saw limited activity in offshore trading. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030102dz12000g7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
