\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Rupiah Ends Down On Import-Related Dlr Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2002\par 10:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended slightly lower Tuesday in thin, pre-holiday trade as some local companies bought dollars to pay for their import bills, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,900 rupiah, up slightly from its close Monday at IDR8,885. \par \par \uc2  State-owned oil and gas company Pertamina was said to be the main buyer of dollars as it was paying for fuel imports to meet rising fuel demand during the holiday season. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially hit an intraday high of IDR8,925 but suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely curbed the greenback's rise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overall the market was quiet as most participants were already on holiday for the Christmas and New Year season, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR8,880 and IDR8,900 Friday with volumes expected to thin further. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, Bank Indonesia cut the benchmark weighted average rate of its one-month Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes to 12.99% in a weekly auction from 13.02% last week. \par \par \uc2  The rate cut signals Bank Indonesia is confident that the rupiah won't come under pressure in the near term amid the dollar's global weakness, and suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't too concerned about inflationary pressures in December. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par