\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian rupiah confounds doomsayers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2002\par 05:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 15 (Reuters) - Indonesia has been on the ropes this year as its economy struggled, its credit ratings fell to default levels, privatisation made a bumpy start, and the October bombings in Bali dented hopes of foreign investment inflows. \par \par \uc2  Yet the Indonesian rupiah has emerged as the best performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 2002 by a large margin. \par \par \uc2  In fact, a 17 percent gain year-to-date against the dollar, has made the rupiah second only to the South African rand's 34 percent rise in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In 2002, in terms of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there was really only one place to be and that was in rupiah," said David Simmonds, emerging markets strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the rupiah traded near a three-month high of 8,850 per dollar, supported by the expected sale of a key stake in state telecom firm PT Indosat, Indonesia's leading overseas call operator. \par \par \uc2  The IMF injected more confidence by approving a delayed $365 million loan early this month and last week ratings agency Fitch affirmed, rather than downgraded, the country's credit ratings, citing the government's quick response to the Bali bombings and its commitment to economic reform. \par \par \uc2  HIGH YIELD HELPED \par \par \uc2  Analysts say while Indonesia's economic conditions remain precarious and political stability is fragile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could repeat its 2002 performance next year. \par \par \uc2  Cliff Tan, director of Asian research at Salomon Smith Barney in Singapore, said while a healthy recovery would need interest rates and inflation to come down from around 13 percent and 10 percent respectively, Indonesia's better-than-expected fiscal performance, steady state asset sales and progress on security issues would support the economy and the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "It is not inconceivable that once again the rupiah would be the best performer next year, and even against a tepid recovery, Indonesia's economy may look better by next year-end than it does today," he said. Economists polled by Reuters in October lowered their 2002 growth forecast to 3.5 percent and for next year to 3.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  At the height of the Asian financial crisis in 1997/1998 the rupiah dropped to record lows of almost 17,000 to the dollar. It was shaken again in 2001, knocked to more than 12,000 per dollar on fears capital controls might be imposed. \par \par \uc2  But 2002 saw some unexpected positives. \par \par \uc2  Balance of payments pressure eased as Indonesia worked out debt restructuring deals with bilateral and private creditors. The deal pulled its credit ratings back above default grade, although they are still below investment grade. \par \par \uc2  More dollars flowed into the country from the sale of state assets making the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency a regular player in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Jakarta dealers say. For details on FDI inflows to Asia, click on \par \par \uc2  But more importantly, says Philip Wee, market strategist at DBS Bank in Singapore, the growing interest rate differential between the rupiah and the dollar provided a pillar of support. \par \par \uc2  "Because of the high yield, net exporters kept supplying the market with dollars," he said. \par \par \uc2  With Indonesia's real interest rates around three percent and U.S. rates at four-decade lows, much of Indonesia's $2 billion a month trade surplus was kept in the country. \par \par \uc2  RESILIENT INVESTORS \par \par \uc2  Much of the rupiah's strength this year has come from the resilience of domestic investors, who faced their toughest test after the Bali bombings, in which almost 200 people, mostly foreign tourists, were killed. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah slumped to six-month lows around 9,400 per dollar after the bombings, which raised security fears, in a country hardened to civil strife in recent years, to new highs. \par \par \uc2  Aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the rupiah back towards 9,000, but analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was successful only because domestic investors did not respond to Bali as negatively as had been expected. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that local investors choose not to pull out as forcefully as expected made the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's job relatively easy," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  Salomon's Tan said Jakarta's progress on investigations into the bomb attacks has also surprised most of its critics. \par \par \uc2  "In public, we have seen a noticeable step-up in official commitment to anti-terrorism efforts from the highest levels of government, and closer observers tell us that on the ground such efforts are driven more by national intelligence services than by their overseas critics," Tan said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021215dycf005pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
