\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up; Suspected Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2002\par 10:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended slightly higher Wednesday, recovering from initial losses as state banks allegedly sold U.S. dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at IDR8,975, down from its close Tuesday at IDR8,990. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar first rose to an intraday high of 9,010 rupiah as interbank market participants covered their dollar-short positions ahead of the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia was then believed to have sold dollars to cap the U.S. unit's rise, effectively dragging it down below the psychologically-important IDR9,000 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign banks also sold dollars, likely on behalf of investors that were buying assets held by the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency and needed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for payment, they added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to trade lower to around IDR8,950. Any rise would most likely be capped around IDR9,000. \par \par \uc2  Although the rupiah closed higher, Bank Indonesia left the benchmark weighted average rate of its one-month Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes unchanged at 13.06% in a weekly auction Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's recovery from around IDR9,400 following the deadly Oct. 12 Bali blasts has been providing room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to guide the SBI rate lower, which in helps reduce the costs of its borrowings related to bank bailouts. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the unchanged rate Wednesday signals that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has turned cautious in pushing the rate down further, as concerns over rising inflationary pressures mount. \par \par \uc2  Prices of goods are expected to rise significantly in December, owing to religious and holiday festivities that month. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021127dybr000ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
