\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Easier, Singapore dlr off 7-wk highs. \b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2002\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 1 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar\par was off seven-week highs in early Friday trade as most Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped back from recent peaks ahead of key economic\par data from the United States later in the day.\par     Dealers said that unless trade in major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed a\par clear trend, players in regionals would be reluctant to carry\par long dollar positions over the weekend.\par     Dollar recouped lost ground on the yen        helped by\par demand related to local commercial fixing and talk of buying\par from Japanese trust banks. However, the euro was still holding\par near three-week highs hit in New York.\par     Dealers said players would avoid aggressive moves ahead of\par U.S. October employment report and the Institute for Supply\par Management's manufacturing data due on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been on an uptrend since\par mid-October on improved foreign equity inflows and unwinding of\par long dollar/yen positions -- a corrective phase analysts said\par might be nearing an end.\par     "We think this was a correction in trend, and fundamentally\par remain bearish on Asia. This is in view of persistent macro\par weakness, likely bottoming in dollar/yen and momentum of\par foreign equity market purchases in Asia topping out," said\par Bhanu Baweja, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Singapore.\par     The Singapore dollar touched a fresh seven-week high of\par 1.7640 hit in offshore trade on Thursday inspired by the yen's\par rise to its strongest level in four weeks during the New York\par trading session.\par  \par     But eyeing early Friday's losses in the yen, players sold\par the Singapore dollar down to around 1.7685 per dollar.\par     Reinforcing bearish sentiment this morning was the release\par of Purchasing Managers' Index for October that fell one point\par to 50.6.\par     Although a reading above 50 indicates the manufacturing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sector is generally expanding, but the lower reading from last\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par month showed the trend was peaking. The electronics index fell\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 3.6 points to 49.4 versus September to record a contraction\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par after seven months of expansion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BAHT AWAITS RESERVES DATA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai baht was still struggling at levels just\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par shy of its four-week highs around 43.26, aided by dollar sales\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from exporters and local corporates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Speculation ahead of the release of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par reserves position as of October 25, due 0800 GMT, was also\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par putting downward pressure on the baht.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reserves have been on decline since last month given the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of Thailand's active management of the exchange rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers in Bangkok said they suspected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollars on Thursday to beef up reserves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thailand's foreign reserves stood at $36.7 billion on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par October 18, against $37.1 billion on October 11 and $37.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par billion on October 4.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso was virtually unmoved at around\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 53.05 per dollar in light offshore trade with local markets\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par closed for holidays. Trade will resume on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah was steady at around 9,230 per\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar after Thursday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sold dollars to prop up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers in Jakarta said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold an estimated\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par $40 million to $50 million to push the rupiah to around 9,200,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a level the bank had defended since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took a plunge\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par after the deadly Bali bombing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The South Korean won eased to around 1,2225.4,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from Thursday's high of 1,219.4, its strongest since September\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 23. For detailed report,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Taiwan dollar          eased to around 34.785, from\par Thursday's onshore close of 34.761. For detailed report, click\par on       .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     122.77       122.48         -0.24\par  Sing dlr      1.7686       1.7669         -0.10 #VAL