\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupiah leads rebound, funds sell dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2002\par 11:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 16 (Reuters) - A sharp rebound in the Indonesian rupiah, after its heavy losses following the Bali bombings, and heavy profit-booking by U.S. investment houses sparked a rally in Asian regionals on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also helped by a five percent rally in U.S. stocks, taking gains in the last four sessions to over 13 percent, that boosted regional share markets, although disappointing results from Intel Corp limited gains. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a firmer yen after the U.S. dollar's failure to hold its gains above 125 yen, a high since June, was an added plus. The yen was around 124.25 per dollar in late trade. \par \par \uc2  "Rallying stocks and the firmer yen is underpinning a broad-based Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovery with several markets showing signs of major reversal," Lehman Brothers strategist Ron Leven said in his market commentary. \par \par \uc2  As some of the bearishness related to regional security and economic risks ebbed, the rupiah rallied to 9,200 per dollar, regaining half the losses incurred after the bombings. \par \par \uc2  Some state banks were reported to have sold dollars, suspected to be on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and Tuesday to prop up the rupiah at six-month low around 9,400. \par \par \uc2  "Similar to the September 11 attacks, many agreed that business activities and consumers spending would not be adversely affected as long as the Bali blast is an isolated incident," Jimmy Koh, economist at United Overseas Bank, said. \par \par \uc2  "Indeed, most Asian equities chose to ignore the weekend bombing focusing instead on the outperforming U.S. equities." \par \par \uc2  "Overall, other than the short-term financial markets implications, the real sector impact is likely to be contained for now, although there will definitely be an increase in regional risk aversion," Koh said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign fund inflows helped the Taiwan dollar bounce from an 11-month low of 35.268 and close at a two-week high of 34.97. \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar, helped by the rupiah, strengthened to around 1.7950/60, where it traded last Friday, after hitting a five-month low of 1.8079 in offshore trade on Tuesday. The rupiah is thought to be significant in the trade-weighted basket that authorities use to manage the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said heavy position liquidation by U.S.-based investment houses helped drive the rebound in the Sing dollar and the Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  SLUGGISH BAHT, RELUCTANT PESO \par \par \uc2  The baht made a slow start, but soon caught up with other regionals. The baht rallied past 44 per dollar from a 10-month low of 44.26 per dollar in offshore trade. \par \par \uc2  A 1.4 percent gain in Thai stocks and an upgrade of some bank ratings by Fitch also buoyed the baht in later deals. \par \par \uc2  "The baht is firm but lagging and we are thinking of getting long again via options," Leven's report said, adding the implied volatility had not moved from around seven percent for months. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won also erased losses sustained since the Bali blasts, and surged from five-month lows at 1,267 amid talk of foreign direct investment flows. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso came off this week's lows of 53.12, also a low since August last year, following its regional counterparts and reassured by comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Amando Tetangco on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Tetangco said the government was expecting substantial remittances from overseas workers in the fourth quarter. He also advised against speculation and requesting those with remittances to convert those into pesos. \par \par \uc2  But traders said worries about the fiscal deficit had resurfaced. Media reports quoted a government official saying the deficit could surge to 180 billion pesos ($3.4 billion) in 2002. \par \par \uc2  Government sources have said the deficit for the first nine months of 2002 is around 160 billion pesos, above the revised annual target of about 155 billion pesos. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0830 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Japan yen     124.23       124.68         +0.36\par   Sing dlr      1.7963       1.8070         +0.60\par   Taiwan dlr    34.970       35.168         +0.57\par   Korean won   1246.90      1263.40         +1.32\par   Baht           43.92        44.18         +0.59\par   Peso           52.89        53.08         +0.36\par   Rupiah       9200.00      9260.00         +0.65\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     124.23       131.24         +5.64\par   Sing dlr      1.7963       1.8515         +3.07\par   Taiwan dlr    34.970       34.986         +0.05\par   Korean won   1246.90      1277.00         +2.41\par   Baht           43.92        44.16         +0.55\par   Peso           52.89        51.50         -2.63\par   Rupiah       9200.00     10350.00        +12.50 ((Vidya\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Ranganathan, Asia Treasury Desk (65) 6870-3090\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par email:vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021016dyag00jav\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\