\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA DEBT: Indonesia Dlr Bonds Swoon After Bali Blast \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 1,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2002\par 12:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The fallout from the weekend bombings in Bali reverberated through Indonesia's debt market Tuesday, hammering dollar-denominated bonds especially hard. \par \par \uc2  The bomb blast shattered what little was left of investor confidence in Indonesia, and it further dimmed the nation's economic outlook, with the tourism industry expected to feel the brunt of the terrorist attack. \par \par \uc2  "The near-term implications on the economy will likely be harsh, as the latest incident puts to an end the hitherto improving trend in underlying macro fundamentals," J.P. Morgan Chase Bank said in a report. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. investment bank's estimates point to a possible loss of up to $2.7 billion in tourism earnings over the next 12 months - accounting for 1.3% of the country's annual gross domestic product and nearly half of the projected current account surplus. \par \par \uc2  The government's policy response will be crucial in reviving confidence among international investors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian government has blamed al-Qaida for the attacks that ripped through nightclubs on the Indonesian resort island on Saturday night, killing almost 200 people and injuring many more. \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's Ratings Services warned late Monday the bomb blast "could adversely affect Indonesia's economy," although the international ratings agency maintained the sovereign long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating at CCC-plus, with a stable outlook. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's bonds were marked lower Tuesday as bond market participants from Hong Kong and Tokyo returned from their three-day weekend. Still, the bonds were barely traded. \par \par \uc2  "Dealers are marking them lower because they're not sure where the customers want to buy," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The extent of the fall in prices was especially stark in the bids that were quoted in the market, as those with any interest in buying bided their time, waiting for lower levels. Earlier Tuesday, the offer spreads on Indonesian corporate credits widened some 50-60 basis points, while bids blew out even more by 100-120 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian bond prices consequently fell by two or three points compared with Friday's levels. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian sovereign 7.75% bond due 2006 was quoted at 103-104. \par \par \uc2  Among Indonesian corporate credits, the more liquid Indofood 10.375% paper was quoted at 101-102. \par \par \uc2  "We think that net selling from foreign investors is likely, though we expect local players to step up on selective names, given that local liquidity remains strong and supply pipeline from Indonesia issuers would appear more unlikely now," UBS Warburg said in a report. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan Chase reiterated its investment stance on Indonesia: "steer clear of Indonesia's sovereign debt given its unattractive risk-reward tradeoff." \par \par \uc2  At the heart of the debt market's concerns is the potentially damaging impact a weaker rupiah could have on the country's debt burden. \par \par \uc2  The bombings in Bali roiled Indonesian financial markets, sending the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reeling by more than 4% to a fresh six-month low of 9,400 rupiah to the dollar earlier Tuesday. The dollar had been quoted around IDR9,005 as of late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the rupiah's slide, analysts expect the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken further. \par \par \uc2  A weaker rupiah would make it harder to service Indonesia's $131.2 billion in foreign debt, of which some $71 billion is government debt and about $60 billion is private, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Even after having undergone restructuring, many companies still haven't converted their outstanding dollar debt to rupiah, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "For a company that has a lot of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, they're more vulnerable," said Prasoon Dayal, a Hong Kong-based credit analyst at J.P. Morgan. A weaker rupiah would increase their costs, hurting their bottom line. \par \par \uc2  "On the pure revenue side, I would imagine less companies will be directly affected," given that domestic demand will likely remain buoyant, said Dayal. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian corporations that will be hurt by the Bali aftermath include those that need to raise equity in order to recapitalize their balance sheet. Also, companies with a significant amount of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt relative to cash flow, short-term refinancing needs or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -sensitivity to cash flow will be vulnerable, UBS Warburg said. \par \par \uc2  The fundamental impact on companies like cellular operator Telkomsel, Satelindo, Freeport Indonesia, Medco Energi and APP Group would be neutral, according to analysts at UBS Warburg. These companies have either sufficient financial cushions or revenues denominated in dollars, they added. \par \par \uc2  The impact on Indofood Sukses Makmur would be moderately negative, according to the investment bank. \par \par \uc2  "While revenues should be resilient, we expect profit margins to come under pressure from the weaker rupiah," said UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Some 88% of the food company's revenue is denominated in rupiah, while 60% of production and distribution costs are denominated in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The impact on cement makers Indocement Tunggal Prakarsa and Semen Cibinong will be moderately negative as domestic demand for cement will likely decline, UBS Warburg said. \par \par \uc2  Both companies also have mainly dollar-denominated debt, though backing from their respective foreign parent companies and a favorable debt amortization profile should mitigate the fallout from the Bali blast, the bank added. \par \par \uc2  Car maker Astra International will be particularly vulnerable to the fallout from Bali because of its dollar debt and its ongoing debt restructuring. Most of the component cost of the automobiles and motorcycles the company manufactures are denominated in dollars, so the fundamental impact would be negative, UBS Warburg noted. \par \par \uc2  But Dayal at J.P. Morgan is more sanguine about the prospects for these Indonesian companies notwithstanding the potential Bali aftermath. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupiah has been relatively resilient for most of the year, making\par it easier for companies to service debts, so a brief pullback in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to create a huge problem, he said. The rupiah has\par been the world's third strongest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year, having\par appreciated some 11.5% against the dollar since the end of 2001.\par    -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021015dyaf000mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
