\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Firm Against Yen, Euro and Pound in Subdued Trading \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2002\par 00:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newsiwres \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar put in a mixed performance Monday -- edging moderately higher against the yen and modestly lower against the Swiss franc -- following the weekend bombing on the Indonesian Island of Bali, though more localized \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Australian and New Zealand dollars did see some selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was trading at 124.26 yen, up from 124.03 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was worth around 98.67 U.S. cents, unchanged from late Friday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was worth about 1.4810 Swiss francs, down from 1.4831 francs Friday in New York. Sterling was changing hands at $1.5572, down from $1.5614 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Aside from slight weakness against the classic safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the Swiss franc -- perhaps the most remarkable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaction to Saturday's blasts was the fact that the dollar barely reacted at all. \par \par \uc2  Even with the hike in risk aversion following the Bali attack -- which President Bush said appeared to be linked to al Qaeda -- the greenback strengthened against the yen and the pound and held its ground against the euro. \par \par \uc2  No doubt partly accounting for the dollar's resilience were the extremely thin trading conditions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as many financial professionals were out of the office with holidays in Canada, the U.S. and Japan. But analysts said the dollar's lack of movement also stemmed from a sense that other terrorist events had to a certain extent, been expected. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has already priced in a lot risk of this kind of thing," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at UBS Warburg, who asked not to be named. "That supports the view that [the Bali bombing] is not being seen as a sign of heightened global risk." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also likely reverting to its traditional role as a magnet for investors in times of global turmoil. \par \par \uc2  With sliding stock markets around the world and the growing threat of a U.S.-led war on Iraq, investors were increasingly risk averse ahead of the Bali explosion in any case. This has kept the ripple effects from the devastating bomb attack largely within the region. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah crumbled to its weakest level in almost six months, while the attack also sent shock waves through regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and knocked more than 10% off Jakarta stocks. The blast also initially supported gold and oil prices. The rupiah, which is barely traded in New York, was quoted at 9,330 Indonesian rupiah late in the day, up from 9,010 rupiah Friday, but off an intraday peak of 9,350 rupiah after Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rupiah's slide. \par \par \uc2  Explosions ripped through a nightclub district in southern Bali Saturday night, leaving at least 188 dead and hundreds more injured. Investors' rattled nerves over the blast dragged down \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets in Asia, as well as shares in Europe. That tension carried over initially also into Wall Street in early trade, although the broad market indexes managed to end the day in positive territory. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the attack could pose longer-term threats to the Australian and New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , potentially hampering tourism and export demand. Australia, because of its proximity to Indonesia and the large number of Australians among the casualties, is particularly vulnerable, some said. "It will have some effect on the [Australian] economy," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Morgan Stanley in New York. "Certainly you would expect a knock on consumer confidence." \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was trading at 54.64 U.S. cents late Monday, down from 54.95 cents late Friday. The New Zealand dollar was worth 47.86 U.S. cents, down from 48.10 cents late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the other key focus for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was Brazil, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an extraordinary meeting and hiked interest rates by three percentage points, in a move that analysts said seemed designed to shore up the real after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to an all-time low around 4.104 Brazilian reals last week. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately this is a stopgap measure" to help tide the real over until the new presidential administration takes office in Brazil next year, said Steven Leach, emerging markets strategist at Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, the real failed to take heart from the rate hike, ending the day weaker as analysts questioned the implications of the surprise rate hike, both for Brazil's debt servicing costs and for the real economy. Late Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at 3.860 Brazilian reals to the dollar, weaker than 3.820 reals late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "This interest rate hike is unlikely to do much [that's] constructive, apart from lingering the growth slowdown," said Yianos Kontopoulos, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York. "I don't understand why they needed to do it now as opposed to a week from now. I wouldn't even understand it then." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision came nine days before a regularly scheduled meeting, leaving market participants confused as to why it had rushed the rate hike now, particularly given the real's apparent lack of sensitivity to interest rates. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suffering from a confidence issue and 300 basis points will have no meaning here," said Douglas Smith, chief economist for the Americas at Standard Chartered in New York. "The bias for the real to weaken will be unaffected," by the rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision now further complicates the prospects of government-backed hopeful Jose Serra in a second-round presidential vote on Oct. 27. Serra is already trailing in a distant second place in opinion polls to Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva of the left-leaning Workers' Party. \par \par \uc2  -- Grainne McCarthy contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021014dyae007pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
