\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late;Rupiah At 5-Mo Low On Bombing \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 960 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2002\par 12:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah crumbled to a five-month low Monday after the weekend's fatal bomb attacks in Bali, which sent shock waves through regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and roiled the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  The incident, which killed at least 188 people, heightened risk aversion in Asia and drove the Singapore dollar, the Thai baht and the Philippine peso to levels unseen in several months. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,330 rupiah, up from IDR9,010 Friday, but off an intraday peak of IDR9,350 after Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rupiah's slide. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key stock index plunged 10.4% to 337.475, its lowest since April 1998, though other markets were mixed. Singapore shares climbed 0.4%, Korean shares jumped 4.6%, and Taiwan's stock index rose 1.6%, but Thailand's key stock index fell nearly 2%, Malaysian stocks lost 1.2%, and Philippine shares fell 0.4%. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia said it will continue to monitor the rupiah and will act if necessary to keep its level stable. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be hard-pressed to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid mounting security concerns. \par \par \uc2  The bombings added to fears voiced by the U.S. government recently that Indonesia - known for its relatively weak law enforcement - has become a base for international terrorism with links to al-Qaida. \par \par \uc2  Ratings agency Standard & Poor's said the bomb blasts could hurt tourism, a major revenue earner for Indonesia. The agency said the government will have to walk a fine line to win both domestic and international support to regain confidence in the country and its government. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see further downside to the rupiah going forward as jitters over global terrorism prompt investors to shun this region. \par \par \uc2  Chong Hui Chin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank, said the incident "reinforces our view that things are not going to look up (for Indonesia) in the next three months." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. investment bank, which had previously been upbeat about Indonesia, last week revised its dollar/rupiah target to 10,000 by year end, from 8,700 previously. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America said any attempts by the government to clamp down on terror groups could induce a further backlash from them and dramatically increase domestic security risks. \par \par \uc2  "The perception of such risks is likely to damage Indonesia's capability to attract much needed foreign capital and hurt its already fragile economy," the bank said in a note. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the incident in Bali spooked a market that was already jittery after a bomb blast killed six people in the southern Mindanao island Friday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pierced the key 53-peso resistance to end at 53.030 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, its highest close since Aug. 8, 2001, and up from PHP52.750 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Some traders suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at PHP52.990 and PHP53.030 earlier in the session to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from staging a convincing break of the PHP53-level. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced off early highs against the Singapore dollar and was quoted around S$1.7986 late in Asia, from S$1.7934 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar jumped to S$1.8022, its highest since late-May \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, early demand for dollars dragged the baht to its lowest since end-January, though this was tempered after exporters sold the U.S. unit when it reached its intraday peak of 44.03 baht. \par \par \uc2  But later in the day, dollar buying by local oil companies and importers pushed the dollar up again to close the Asian session at 44.02 baht, up from THB43.875 Friday. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day as the baht's weakness was minimal compared with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said the baht's movement was minimal and in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements, but thinks the effects from the Bali bombings will be limited. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said the attacks may have an indirect impact on Thailand's tourism industry. \par \par \uc2  Previous security incidents in Indonesia and Philippines have resulted in tourists diverting their travel plans to Thailand, which has a reputation for greater stability and less strife. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the New Taiwan Dollar extended losses to close at a fresh low since January 1987 as importers and interbank players bought U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said market players are concerned the Bali bomb blasts will add further gloom to the already murky outlook for the region's economy. \par \par \uc2  But volume was thin with market players mostly sidelined as the Japan and Hong Kong markets were closed for holidays. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$35.168, up from NT$35.157. Friday, but off an intraday high of NT$35.21 as offshore non-deliverable forward players took profit on the dollar after modest foreign equity inflows. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was relatively steady with trade at a trickle since Tokyo markets were shut for a holiday and U.S. markets will be closed later in the global day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,259.3 won, slightly down from KRW1,259.5 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The rally on the local bourse helped calm sentiment toward the won, which was pressured in recent days by plunging share prices, said Oh Suktae, economist at Citibank/Salomon Smith Barney. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Ditas Lopez in Manila, and the Bangkok and Indonesian bureaus, contributed to this item.) \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021014dyae000k2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
