\par\par \b \uc2  Bali Blasts Deal Blow To Confidence In Southeast Asia \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 1,106 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2002\par 10:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The deadly weekend bombings in Bali have dealt a major blow not only to Indonesia's economy but to efforts across southeast Asia to win back investor confidence after economic setbacks in the past five years. \par \par \uc2  Businessmen and analysts worry that the massive car bomb which tore apart a nightclub on the Indonesian resort island Saturday night, killing at least 188 people and injuring hundreds more, indicates Indonesia has become a base for international terrorism with links to al-Qaida. \par \par \uc2  If so, that could mean further such attacks in coming months, not only in Indonesia but in the Philippines, which is fighting a Muslim secessionist movement, or Malaysia and Singapore, which have had to act against terrorist groups since last year's September 11 attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "This just puts an exclamation point on the concept that southeast Asia is a hotbed of international terrorism," said Dick Baker, Indonesia specialist at the East-West Center in Honolulu. \par \par \uc2  Although nobody had claimed responsibility for the attacks by Monday, the Bali bombings significantly expanded the violence in Indonesia beyond previous attacks, which were carried out mainly by separatist movements, Baker added. \par \par \uc2  "This has all the earmarks of an international terrorist attack," he said. \par \par \uc2  If the idea of southeast Asia as a terrorist danger spot becomes entrenched among U.S., European and Japanese investors, analysts said, capital flows into southeast Asia will shrink further, some institutions may pull long-term money out, and trade and tourism could suffer serious damage. \par \par \uc2  "This comes at a very bad time for everyone," said Paul Schymyck, regional economist at IDEAglobal in Singapore. "With a possible double-dip recession coming in the U.S., this is the last thing you need." \par \par \uc2  Indonesian markets plunged Monday in response to the bombings. The Jakarta stock index lost 10% to its lowest levels since April 2001, while the rupiah tumbled from Friday's close of 9,010 against the dollar to 9,350, before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its losses. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the rupiah could drop as low as 10,000 by the end of this year if security fears persisted, and in Jakarta on Monday such fears were widespread, especially after the U.S. embassy said it was sending non-essential staff out of the country. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian officials warned foreign energy firms could be the next targets. "There is information that the energy sector will be targeted by terrorists," Indonesia's senior security minister, Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono, told reporters, pledging to step up protection of such investments. \par \par \uc2  Markets Outside Indonesia Quite Calm \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, however, markets were much calmer. Although the Singapore dollar sank to a four-month low of 1.8030 against the U.S. dollar in the opening minutes of trade from Friday's 1.7835 close, it had come well off that low by afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso and Thai baht also fell, and the Australian dollar slipped amid concern that the terrorists could be targeting Australian citizens; many of the victims in Bali were Australian tourists. But these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't drop as much as the Singapore dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in northern Asia were stable. \par \par \uc2  In Asian stock markets, there was selling of individual stocks with large business interests in Indonesia or exposure to southeast Asian tourism, particularly airlines. Australia's Qantas lost 2.5%, and Singapore Airlines had dropped 2.9% by late afternoon. \par \par \uc2  But outside Jakarta, stock markets weren't sold across the board, and several actually rose on the back of Wall Street's strong rebond on Friday. Sydney's S&P/ASX 200 rose 1.2% and Singapore's main index was up 0.6% in late trade; "temporarily funds exiting Indonesia are seeing Singapore as a safer place," a foreign broking house trader said. \par \par \uc2  Asian U.S. dollar bond spreads widened slightly in response to the Bali attacks but trade was thin, partly because of Monday's closure of Hong Kong, Tokyo and the U.S. Treasurys market for holidays. \par \par \uc2  HSBC economist John Edwards said the uncertainty created by the Bali bombings had probably eradicated any remaining chance of Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising rates at its next board meeting on November 5. But he added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely retained its tightening bias, given economic factors such as Australia's housing boom. \par \par \uc2  Fund manager William Pitman at Henderson Global Investors, which has about US$2 billion invested in Asia, said it was too soon to think there would be an exodus of foreign money from southeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  "It really depends whether (terrorism) is perceived to be more of an endemic problem, which it has probably not proved to be yet," he said, adding that ultimately, economics would drive the stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Economies Vulnerable To Confidence Shocks \par \par \uc2  But the region's economies are vulnerable to any confidence shock, analysts noted. Singapore last week reported growth in the third quarter that was well below expectations. The Philippine economy is coming under increasing pressure from its growing budget deficit, and Malaysia's export sector is threatened by a possible slowdown in the event of a U.S. invasion of Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Foreign companies, which are already directing a larger portion of their overseas investment to China, may now have one more reason to avoid southeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The bomb blasts illustrate the risks involved in many countries in Asia, particularly southeast Asia," said Steve Brice, chief economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Bali attacks may reinforce the perception that China and northeast Asian economies such as South Korea and Taiwan are better placed in terms of their risk-return profile than southeast Asia, he said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore, a traditional safe haven in Southeast Asia, "may now appear more vulnerable", Brice added. \par \par \uc2  Song Seng Wun, regional economist at securities house G.K. Goh in Singapore, said the Bali attacks were unlikely to have any long-term impact on Asian economies unless they were followed by more terrorist incidents. \par \par \uc2  But if there is a string of further attacks, there will be negative implications across a range of commercial activities, in tourism, the services sector, the travel industry and domestic consumption, he said. \par \par \uc2  As it is, the Bali tragedy may mean an expected lift in regional economies in the fourth quarter of this year could be less than originally expected, even though economic indicators will have support from their low bases during the post-September 11 period last year. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021014dyae000f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
