\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  At Multi-Mo Lows After Bali Bomb Blasts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2002\par 08:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- -The weekend's fatal bomb blast in Bali dragged the Indonesian rupiah to its lowest level in five months and roiled the stock market Monday, sending shock waves through regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tumbled to near five-month lows, the Thai baht dived to an eight-month trough and the Philippine peso crashed to a 14-month low. \par \par \uc2  Traders said what appeared to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped limit the dollar's rise in that market, but it was likely to be little more than a temporary respite, as jitters over global terrorism prompt investors to shun this region. \par \par \uc2  Around 0520 GMT, the dollar was quoted around 9,263 rupiah, up from IDR9,010 at the Asian close Friday, but off its intraday peak of IDR9,335, which was its highest level since early-May. \par \par \uc2  The Jakarta Stock Exchange Composite index was down 9.1% to 342.122. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the dollar was quoted around S$1.7990, up from S$1.7935 late Friday, and at PHP52.95, up from its PHP52.75 close Friday. \par \par \uc2  Regional stock markets were faring better, with Singapore's STI up 0.8% at 1385 and Taiwan's benchmark index up more than 1% at 3910.98. \par \par \uc2  Stock markets in Malaysia and Thailand were both slightly lower, while Korea's KOSPI index was up nearly 5% at 615.60. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong and Tokyo financial markets were closed for holidays. \par \par \uc2  Saturday's car-bomb attack on a popular nightspot in Bali, which killed at least 188, marks the first large-scale terrorist attack directed at foreigners in Indonesia. Two other bombs went off almost simultaneously, one at a U.S. consular office in Bali and the other near the Philippine consulate on Sulawesi island, but there were no casualties in those attacks. \par \par \uc2  Although no group immediately claimed responsibility for the attacks, the bombings added to fears voiced by the U.S. government recently that Indonesia - known for its relatively weak law enforcement - has become a base for international terrorism with links to al-Qaida. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the weekend's events support their views that there's little to be positive about Indonesia going forward. \par \par \uc2  Just last week, J.P. Morgan Chase Bank, which had previously been upbeat about the country, sent out notes to clients advising them to cut rupiah positions. \par \par \uc2  Chong Hui Chin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the bank, said J.P. Morgan has also revised its dollar/rupiah target to 10,000 by year end, from 8,700 previously. \par \par \uc2  That revision was made just before the weekend's events, and it "reinforces out view that things are not going to look up (for Indonesia) in the next three months," she said. \par \par \uc2  Irene Cheung, sovereign and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro Bank in Singapore, said the blasts were a wake up call to regional governments, and may prompt more cooperation among the countries in handling the threat. \par \par \uc2  But in the near term, it adds to the broad market uncertainty and will likely keep investors sidelined on Asia, she said. \par \par \uc2  The terrorist attacks are expected to have a negative impact on travel in the region. Japan's Foreign Ministry was quick to issue a warning for Japanese nationals in Indonesia to stay away from crowded places as much as possible. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said the attacks in Bali may have an indirect impact on Thailand's tourism industry, but it remains to be seen to what extent there would be a reduction in travel overall. \par \par \uc2  He said the baht should continue to move in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The baht doesn't need to weaken more or even as much as the rest of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because we aren't suffering any (direct) impact," Thaksin told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "The baht must move in line with the region. If the baht weakens or strengthens in line with the region it is no problem for us," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading just under THB44 at midday, off an early eight-month high of THB44.03 but up from THB43.875 at the Asian close Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar extended losses from Friday's weakest closing since January 1987, though this was offset by some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  At midday, the dollar was traded at NT$35.171, up from NT$35.157 Friday, but off a high of NT$35.210 earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was relatively stable, with the dollar quoted at KRW1,258.5, down slightly from KRW1,259.5 Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021014dyae0008v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
