\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late On Yen, Oil, US Ports Closure \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2002\par 12:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurtled to multiple-month lows Monday, driven by a sagging yen and falling equity markets, with Japan's Nikkei 225 Stock Average hitting a new 19-year trough. \par \par \uc2  Higher oil prices on news of an explosion on a French oil tanker off eastern Yemen early Sunday and the continued closure of U.S. West Coast ports due to a labor dispute took further toll on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The ports shutdown, which comes in the run-up to the critical Christmas shopping season, could hurt Asian exports as these ports are the main entry points to key U.S. markets. \par \par \uc2  It adds to existing uncertainty about global economic growth, especially that of the U.S., where latest data continues to send mixed signals, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There's so much of uncertainties plaguing the markets at the moment. There's a whole list of risks," said Claudio Piron, Singapore-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed at its weakest level against the dollar since May 24, reflecting the yen's drop and the sharp fall on the local bourse, where the key index plunged 3.6%. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which was also buoyed by offshore buying, closed at 1,240.9 won, up from KRW1,232.4 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Participants continued to buy dollars on jitters over the stock market's weakness and the dollar/yen's rise," said A.R. Park, a dealer at Shinhan Bank. "They are buying dollars or delaying their dollar sales on anticipation of further gains in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Uncertainties over the global economy and the possibility of a war in Iraq have caused players to hold on to or buy dollars recently, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso extended last week's losses to close at yet another 14-month low, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Amando Tetangco reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness is likely only temporary. \par \par \uc2  He told Dow Jones Newswires that the peso has been pressured by general weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also dollar short-covering by banks. \par \par \uc2  He said the peso is expected to be lifted toward year-end by seasonally stronger remittances from Filipinos working abroad. \par \par \uc2  Tetangco said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been selling dollars to support the peso, but is monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent any excess volatility. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at its session peak of 52.900 pesos, up from PHP52.725 Friday, and its highest close since Aug. 9, 2001. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said demand for dollars was mainly coming from oil companies bracing for higher crude prices globally. \par \par \uc2  Declining stocks and weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dragged the New Taiwan dollar to a seven-month closing low. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's main stock index fell 3.5% to a fresh 11-month low, prompting traders to switch from the local dollar to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at NT$35.069, its highest close since March 5 and up from NT$34.994 Friday. It traded between NT$35.046 and NT$35.089 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht tumbled to a six-month low despite two apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered at around 43.54 baht at the Asian open before a run up fueled by offshore players' adjustments to keep the local unit in line with regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also demand from local oil importers. \par \par \uc2  Oil importers bought dollars as global oil prices rose following the oil tanker explosion off the coast of Yemen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's steep rise against the yen also convinced offshore players to buy the greenback. Most of the active players were European banks," a local bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  "A Japanese bank in Singapore, which usually acts on behalf of BOT (Bank of Thailand), bought the baht two times at THB43.62 and THB43.72. But the orders were too weak to make much difference," the trader added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a six-month peak of THB43.80 before closing the Asian session a tad lower at THB43.78, but up sharply from THB43.51 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as the weaker yen triggered sporadic bouts of aggressive U.S. dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  Market participants also started to take a more cautious tone against the local dollar ahead of advance estimates of third-quarter gross domestic product data Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened the Asian session at around S$1.7825, and then rose sharply to breach the psychological S$1.7900 resistance level, sparked by its gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The jump caught some market participants holding short U.S.-dollar positions, sparking further buying to drive it to a session high of S$1.7916. \par \par \uc2  But volume was thin and liquidity tight, with only a few dominant interbank market participants trading actively in the market. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around S$1.7902, from S$1.7804 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was again suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the Indonesian rupiah steady after the long weekend, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia is believed to have been in the market almost daily since late last month. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Overall trading was thin as the market still lacked fresh incentives, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,015 rupiah, unchanged from Thursday, after trading between IDR9,012 and IDR9,020. The markets were closed for a public holiday Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021007dya7000kn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
