\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up; Suspected Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2002\par 11:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended higher Wednesday as the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have once again sold dollars to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,005 rupiah, down from its close at IDR9,050 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank Indonesia was suspected to have been actively selling dollars in a bid to drive the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back below the psychologically important level of IDR9,000. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did slide to an intraday low of IDR8,995, but later staged a partial rebound as local companies accumulated the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on dips, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local companies - which are saddled with about $60 billion in offshore debts - bought dollars to pay maturing offshore debts and to finance imports. Such demand for dollars often exerts pressure on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help the rupiah. However, the bank doesn't comment on specific actions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that they expect corporate dollar demand to continue to rise as the end of the month draws near, when local companies will have to pay maturing offshore debts. \par \par \uc2  They expect the dollar to trade between IDR8,980 and IDR9,025 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Bank Indonesia cut the benchmark weighted average rate of its one-month Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes to 13.22% in a weekly auction from 13.50% last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rate cut signaled Bank Indonesia's intention to drive the rate lower to around 12% by the end of the year to help reduce the costs of the banking sector bailout from the Asian financial crisis of 1997-98. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020925dy9p000l4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
