\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late On Dlr Demand, Econ Concerns \b0\par\par\uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2002\par 13:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid against the U.S. dollar late Friday as market players covered short-dollar positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported demand for dollars from oil companies hedging against higher oil prices. Concerns about foreign equity outflows from the region also weighed on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Worries about the pace of economic recovery in the region were reignited after disappointing data from the U.S., and rather downbeat remarks on the economy from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  But continued uncertainty about a U.S. led war against Iraq, and its impact on oil prices means market players will be more inclined to hold dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There's a bit of risk premium building in," noted Bhanu Baweja, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht and the Singapore dollar were among the biggest casualties Friday. \par \par \uc2  Demand for dollars from oil companies, and short-covering by market players holding dollar/baht options dragged down the baht, a senior trader at a Singapore-based Asian bank said. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices have risen in recent days following mounting tensions between the U.S. and Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around 42.695 baht, from 42.522 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, U.S. dollar purchases by foreign funds pulled down the local dollar as low as S$1.7656 - a level last seen on Aug. 10 - during the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at SS$1.7645, from $S1.7592 previously. \par \par \uc2  Growing concerns that a prolonged war with Iraq would hurt the economy also weighed on the local dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Security Fears Weigh On Rupiah \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit rose against the Philippine peso as banks covered short dollar positions, and oil companies bought dollars to hedge import requirements amid mounting U.S. tensions with Iraq, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 52.230 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP51.970 Thursday. This was the dollar's strongest finish since Aug. 26. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura said the peso's weakness was driven by both external and domestic factors. \par \par \uc2  "Part of it is the possible war in Iraq," he said, adding that the latest kidnapping case in Manila, the country's capital, is also weighing on the peso. \par \par \uc2  He was referring to the kidnapping of the son and daughter of a Philippine congressman earlier Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars also increased as some companies raised in advance their dollar requirements for the end of the quarter. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, dollar sales by exporters, and the yen's firmer tone overnight caused the U.S. unit to slip below 1,200 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  But a flurry of dollar buying by onshore players later in the session sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, forcing many interbank players to cover their short-dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at KRW1,203.8, up from KRW1,201.4 Thursday, after trading in a tight range between KRW1,198.5 and KRW1,204.5 during the day. \par \par \uc2  "We expect no big movements in the dollar in global markets, which means the trading range could be even narrower" next week, possibly between KRW1,195 and KRW1,205, said Oh Suktae, an economist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Indonesian companies bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge their dollar exposure amid concerns over tensions between the U.S. and Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The market remained on tenterhooks as the U.S. kept its embassy closed in Jakarta Friday for the fourth day due to security threats from unidentified groups thought to be linked to al-Qaida. \par \par \uc2  The market is also concerned U.S. companies in Indonesia may face attacks from militant Muslim groups if the U.S. attacks Iraq, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have sold the dollar in the market earlier in the day to defend the rupiah, but the amount wasn't enough to curb the U.S. unit's rise. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to support the rupiah but it doesn't confirm specific \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,965 rupiah, up from IDR8,915 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, a late spate of U.S. dollar buying by importers kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady against the local dollar, which had earlier tracked the yen's firmer tone. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar often tracks the yen because Taiwan and Japan compete in some export categories. Taiwan also imports large amounts of goods from Japan. \par \par \uc2  The Taipei stock market's 1.5% decline also hurt sentiment toward the local dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at NT$34.410, unchanged from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020913dy9d000p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
