\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late On Yen, Risk Aversion \b0\par\par\uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2002\par 11:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- A softer yen drove Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly lower Wednesday, with the New Taiwan dollar falling to its weakest level since late May. \par \par \uc2  However, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to claw out of their intraday troughs as profit-taking on the dollar helped trim losses. \par \par \uc2  Volumes were thin as market players opted to wait out the first anniversary of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Risk aversion was the main theme amid growing prospects of a U.S.-led war on Iraq and the attendant spike in oil prices, with market players unwilling to be caught short of dollars. \par \par \uc2  "There has been some risk aversion that has favored a strong dollar. The headline risks are quite high on the fundamental side. The biggest headline risk is the potential conflict in Iraq," said Vincent Low, Singapore-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 /bond strategy for Asia at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  Economic concerns remained a key concern as higher oil prices, if sustained, will dent economic recovery in an export-dependent region, which is already trying to deal with the prospects of slower growth in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  A UBS Warburg report rated the Philippine peso, the Korean won and the Thai baht as the most vulnerable among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a sustained rise in oil prices \par \par \uc2  With uncertainties both on the geopolitical and economic front, market players were unwilling to take strong positions, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's (trading) mostly done for small amounts, to square off positions," with the dollar's gains capped by profit-taking, said a senior regional trader at an Asian bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at NT$34.395 - its highest since May 24 - from NT$34.303 Tuesday, but off an intraday high of NT$34.403. \par \par \uc2  A dealer said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped support the local dollar, which took its cue from the yen; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between US$150 million and US$200 million to help support the local unit. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar often tracks the yen because Taiwan and Japan compete in some export categories. Taiwan also imports large amounts of goods from Japan. \par \par \uc2  Trading was light, with dealings valued at US$371 million, down from US$486 million previously. \par \par \uc2  Talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped pull the dollar back from intraday highs against the Indonesian rupiah, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but never comments on specific actions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at IDR8,890, up from IDR8,865 the previous day, but off the day's high of IDR8,905. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah didn't react to a statement from Bank Indonesia's board of governors that it hopes to keep cutting rates due to benign inflation, and a stronger rupiah, as market sentiment is barely affected by rate policy. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian authorities are hoping lower rates will spur growth. Finance Minister Boediono said the country was likely to meet its near 4% economic growth target this year. But experts, including the World Bank, warn Indonesia needs at least 6% growth to create enough work for new job seekers each year. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the dollar closed at 1,202.5 won, down from KRW1,198.4 Tuesday but off an intraday high of KRW1,204.0. \par \par \uc2  Like recent sessions, importers and exporters were the main market participants. Importers were buying dollars on dips and exporters were selling them on upticks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Recently, the market cannot find any specific momentum or any direction," said Dong-Ki Jang, chief dealer of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and derivatives at Shinhan Bank. "I expect rangebound trading will continue for the moment - at least until the end of the month." \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed against the Thai baht, but failed to break through the day's high of THB42.50 due to substantial exporter selling orders around that level. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 42.465 baht at the Asian close, up from THB42.41 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit was also up against the Singapore dollar, but off earlier highs as market participants took profits. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around S$1.7551 from S$1.7527 in the previous session. The pair traded between S$1.7555 and S$1.7580 in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso initially tracked the yen but later bucked the regional trend to close slightly firmer. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a lack of corporate demand for dollars allowed the peso to gain ground. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 51.910 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System from PHP51.960 Tuesday, after moving between PHP51.900 and PHP52.080 intraday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020911dy9b000js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
