\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late On Dlr Profit-Taking,US Econ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2002\par 12:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  put up a mixed showing late Tuesday as profit-taking on the U.S. dollar coupled with local factors helped trim or erase early losses. \par \par \uc2  But market players still refrained from taking new positions because of a lack of clear direction from major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the dollar and the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen movements continued to be the dominant factor driving regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and concerns about the U.S. economy remained the main theme that underpinned the performance of Asian units. \par \par \uc2  However, some market participants said there were signs that risk appetite in the region appears to have improved, likely due to an improved equities market in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "That obviously spells better risk appetite and there's a spillover to Asia," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Early in Asia, the yen's weakness weighed down regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as strong overnight gains on Wall Street fueled dollar buying in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar breached key psychological barriers against the Thai baht, the Philippine peso and the New Taiwan dollar in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  But late in the trading day, worries over U.S. trade figures capped the dollar's strength, and helped the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recoup some losses. The U.S. trade data are due later Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was also boosted after credit rating agency Standard & Poor's upgraded the outlook on the country's sovereign ratings to positive, from stable. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar eased against the Thai baht, and the Singapore dollar, and firmed against the New Taiwan dollar, the Philippine peso and the Korean won. The U.S. unit was steady against the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, news of Standard & Poor's upgrade of the outlook for the country's sovereign ratings pulled the dollar back after it broke past the 42 baht level intraday. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the baht was quoted at THB41.935, down from THB41.970 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's raised the outlook on Thailand ratings to positive from stable, citing an improved balance of payments, higher foreign reserves, rising tax revenue and lower corporate debt. But the ratings agency said the country needed to make more progress on debt restructuring for a rating upgrade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell slightly against its Singapore counterpart on thin volumes as ongoing uncertainties about the U.S. economy continued to keep players sidelined. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around S$1.7498, from S$1.7504 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rallied to an intraday high of 8,880 against the Indonesian rupiah on local corporate demand to repay maturing offshore debts and to finance imports. \par \par \uc2  But Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar from nearing the psychologically important IDR9,000 level. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at IDR8,845, marginally below Monday's IDR8,850. \par \par \uc2  A weak yen and mounting concern the government may miss its 2002 budget deficit target of 130 billion pesos continued to weigh on the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing the dollar to stay above the key 52 peso level for most of the day. \par \par \uc2  But by the end of the session, the dollar gave up some of its gains to end at 51.960 pesos, up from PHP51.910 Monday - and a fresh eight-month low for the peso. \par \par \uc2  Monday's resignation of the country's tax chief added to the peso's weakness as many investors viewed it as another blow to the government's efforts to arrest the widening fiscal deficit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended higher against its Taiwan counterpart for the fourth straight session. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at the key NT$34.00 level, up from NT$33.985 Monday, but off an intraday high of NT34.060. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. dollar sales by exporters were offset by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the local unit's rise, as well as importers' demand for the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used about $94 million to nudge the New Taiwan dollar lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the dollar closed at 1,195.3 won, up from KRW1,194.5 Monday, after trading between KRW1,194.0 and KRW1,198.3 intraday in a choppy session. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won tracked the yen's slide, but at the same time, South Korean exporters took profit on the dollar following recent gains. That helped limit the dollar's upside. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020820dy8k000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
