\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Up On Suspected Ctrl Bk Dlr Selling \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2002\par 12:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended higher against the dollar Thursday amid suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,985 rupiah, down from its close Wednesday at IDR9,080. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the fourth consecutive day in an effort to drive the dollar below the psychologically important level of IDR9,000, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They added that Bank Indonesia met little resistance to drive the dollar lower as many market participants were still keen to hold the rupiah due to the large interest rate differential between the dollar and the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The one-month rupiah deposit rate currently is around 14%, whereas the dollar deposit rate is around 1.7%, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Such large interest rate differential makes us feel comfortable to keep the rupiah as long as there is no bad news on Indonesia," said a dealer with a large foreign bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to continue sliding Friday to IDR8,925, with resistance expected at IDR9,025. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was unmoved by news that Indonesia lost out to Australia in a bid to supply 3 million cubic metric tons of gas a year to China's Guangdong province. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia, however, was awarded with a contract to supply 2.5 million tons of gas a year to a new receiving terminal to be constructed in Fujian. Dealers said the Fujian contract should help to raise revenue for the government in the future. \par \par \uc2  The market was also relieved to learn that the nation's highest legislative body, the MPR, is likely to backpedal on recommending the government cuts ties with the International Monetary Fund by 2003. \par \par \uc2  A government official, who declined to be named, told Dow Jones Newswires that the MPR had apparently changed its mind after the government advised the body that such a plan would harm investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  The latest draft of MPR recommendations only suggests that the government be well prepared and armored with economic policies before the $5 billion bailout program with the IMF expires. \par \par \uc2  The draft is expected to be approved in a plenary session later Thursday or Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020808dy88000ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
