\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn On Taiwan-China, US Economy \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2002\par 12:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Mounting China-Taiwan tensions and concerns over a double dip recession in the U.S. pulled most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan markets were the hardest hit, with stocks down almost 6% and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  skirting a key support level against the U.S. dollar, following weekend remarks by Taiwan's President Chen Shui-bian that were interpreted as being pro-independence. \par \par \uc2  However, it was concerns over a double dip recession in the U.S., which gathered momentum after last week's disappointing overall growth, a weak job market and flat manufacturing activity, which pressured other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants said. \par \par \uc2  In his strongest comments since taking office, Chen said over the weekend that Taiwan and China are both countries - a view that is anathema to Chinese leaders who believe the island belongs to China. A civil war split the two sides 53 years ago, and Beijing says the island must unify eventually or face war. \par \par \uc2  Chen also said Taiwan's laws should be amended to allow a national referendum on the island's fate. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has never renounced the use of force to reunify the island under mainland control, and has repeatedly warned that a declaration of independence by the island will prompt a military response. \par \par \uc2  Chen's comments drew an angry response Monday from China, with its top Taiwan affairs spokesman Li Weiyi saying cross-strait relations have been seriously damaged, and that Chen was leading the island to "disaster." \par \par \uc2  Damage control by Taiwan's top China policy maker Tsai Ing-wen, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to calm nerves as investors sold off the New Taiwan dollar and cashed out of equities. \par \par \uc2  "It's (regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weak because of) Taiwan, and basically taking the cue from the dollar/yen (which rose late in the Asian trading day). It's pretty much a knee-jerk reaction," said Philip Wee, market strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region are just going to be reactive to developments in the U.S. and things in the region," he added. \par \par \uc2  Despite the late rise, the broad picture for the U.S. dollar is still bearish, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  "Most of the data is pointing to much softer global recovery led by the U.S. A bad U.S. is not good news for Asia," Wee at DBS said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar came close to crashing through a key support level at NT$34.00 to the U.S. dollar, but aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped shore up the local unit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at NT$33.825, up from NT$33.723 Friday, after trading in a range of NT$33.723 and NT$33.980 through the day. Trading was heavy, with volumes rising to US$880.5 million, compared with US$653 million Friday. Traders estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought more than $200 million of the New Taiwan dollar to prop up market confidence. \par \par \uc2  Still, "compared with the stock market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is relatively calm as institutional investors are the major players here," and they're not bailing out en masse, said a foreign bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the South Korean won finished markedly lower as foreigners offloaded local shares for a fourth straight session, and as the yen retreated against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The greenback ended at 1,198.0 won, up from Friday's close of KRW1,184.5, after trading between KRW1,180.5 and KRW1,199.0. \par \par \uc2  The won initially opened stronger thanks to a slight gain by the yen, but soon succumbed to selling pressure as foreigners sold off their equity holdings. Korean shares ended 3.6% lower over the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said more participants are now long on the dollar, suggesting that most expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain further Tuesday and possibly the rest of the week. The dollar will likely continue to be supported by foreigners' selling of Korean shares. \par \par \uc2  "People think it (the dollar) will go to KRW1,200 in the mid term. Below KRW1,187 is not expected because the dollar is very well-bid," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may even test KRW1,200 as early as Tuesday, said dealers. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also buoyed against the Philippine peso as companies seek dollars for import and debt payment requirements, and on expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut overnight rates soon following a set of weak inflation data for July. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura appeared to rule out an immediate rate cut. \par \par \uc2  "They are probably out of their minds," he said of banks bidding up the dollar in anticipation of a narrowing of the gap between local and U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at 51.510 peso, from 51.300 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also strengthened against the Thai baht, which weakened in tandem with the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on strong importer buying of dollars. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that fears of Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ensured the THB42.30 resistance level held. The U.S. dollar was quoted at 42.15 baht in late Asian trading, from THB42.105 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback had a choppy ride against the Singapore dollar as gains from short-covering were eroded by selling on a cautious outlook for the world's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Still a trader at an Asian bank said, "although the sentiment remains bad toward the U.S. dollar, as a base \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it still attracts bargain hunting." \par \par \uc2  The tumble in the Taiwanese equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday amid heightened tensions between the island and China also reinforced the U.S. dollar's image as a safe haven, he said. \par \par \uc2  There are signs some U.S. investment houses have been buying back their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shift asset portfolios back into the U.S., he said. \par \par \uc2  The market also shrugged off a slight improvement in Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index for July, saying the month-on-month growth is too small to be relevant. \par \par \uc2  The PMI rose to 52.3% in July from 52.1% in June on the back of increases in orders and manufacturing output. \par \par \uc2  An index reading above 50% indicates the manufacturing sector is expanding whereas a reading below 50% indicates a contraction. The PMI is an advance indicator of trends in manufacturing activity. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was quoted at S$1.7558 in late Asian trading, from $1.7559 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed flat at 9,080 rupiah as suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  shielded the local unit from the waves of dollar buying across the region. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At one point, the greenback slid to an intraday low of IDR9,050 on\par suspected dollar selling by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But it rebounded steadily\par during the course of the day as market participants bought the greenback\par in line with its rally against other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254;\par nirmala.menon@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020805dy85000mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
