\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late As US Dlr Takes Battering \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 12:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were higher Monday with several shooting to new multi-month highs Monday as confidence in the U.S. dollar was hammered by a new string of accounting revelations from U.S. companies, and after Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's bearish remarks over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The decline in the U.S. dollar reversed last week's gains and surprised many analysts and traders who had expected Wall Street's strong showing Friday to support the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The sharp gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew the ire of several central banks which made the obligatory warnings or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, as in recent days, their efforts failed to break the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  Only the Indonesian rupiah weakened against the U.S. dollar as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't offset corporate purchases of dollars to pay for imports. The Korean won and the Thai baht both ended at multi-month highs, while the Philippine peso, Singapore dollar and New Taiwan dollar closed higher against the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  "People pretty much jumped on the sell dollar bandwagon," said Thio Chin Loo, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas in Singapore, adding that Shiokawa's remarks over the weekend "isn't good for the (dollar's) outlook." \par \par \uc2  At a weekend meeting of European and Asian finance chiefs in Copenhagen, Shiokawa said officials in various countries believed the dollar was in a downtrend, and could fall to its post-Sept. 11 low of Y115.80. \par \par \uc2  Although Shiokawa's comments weren't a major surprise, "he basically just added more fuel," said Philip Wee, market strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. \par \par \uc2  As at 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y119.13 yen, well below Y120.43 late Friday in Tokyo, and Y120.24 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was dealt a further blow by more accounting-related controversies at big U.S. companies, analysts and traders said. \par \par \uc2  Drug giant Merck & Co. (MRK) said it recorded $12.4 billion in revenue from its pharmacy benefits unit over the past three years that it never actually collected. However, Merck said its revenue-recognition policy conforms to generally accepted accounting principles and that it wouldn't affect its net income. \par \par \uc2  That news came soon after energy firms Reliant Resources and Reliant Energy late Friday announced they had amended their revenues downward by more than a combined $15 billion. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the spate of accounting controversies has further overshadowed the dollar's already week outlook. \par \par \uc2  "A lot hinges on the headline news on these issues. For now, the risk is to the downside on the U.S. dollar," said Thio. \par \par \uc2  In Asian trading, the Singapore dollar rose in tandem with the yen, and ahead of the release of advance estimates of second-quarter gross domestic product data Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The data are widely expected to show the city state has emerged from its long recession. A Dow Jones Newswires poll of 11 economists forecasts an average 2.8% on year rise in the city-state's GDP, after contracting for four straight quarters. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's GDP shrank 2% in 2001, its worst performance since independence in 1965. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7625, from S$1.7721 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone's long on dollars and will look to sell on any rallies from here," a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar crashed to a 22-month low and was quoted at THB41.435 late Monday from THB41.645 before. \par \par \uc2  Stop-loss selling by both exporters and offshore players in afternoon trade coupled with the yen's appreciation pulled the U.S. unit to its Monday low, said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Support for the pair is around THB41.35-41.40, with resistance coming at THB41.55, market observers said. \par \par \uc2  A lack of corporate demand for dollars coupled with the greenback's general weakness helped boost the Philipine peso. The dollar ended at PHP50.470, from PHP50.595 Friday. Traders expect the dollar to test support at PHP50.30 in the next few days. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Korean won rode on the yen's rise to a 19-month high against the dollar. The dollar ended at KRW1,191.4 from KRW1,204.9 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that any U.S. dollar bids during the trading session came predominantly from state-run banks, with the majority of market players offloading the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The won strengthened despite another government warning intended to dull the bullish won sentiment. Kwon Tae-shin, director general at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's international bureau, said early Monday market players' "excessive expectations of further won gains based on the yen's recent movements" are undesirable, and that the government will take "active" steps to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar popped up KRW2 immediately after Kwon's remarks, but quickly returned to its downward trajectory since players believe there's little the government can do to stem the dollar's fall given the worldwide trend. \par \par \uc2  As is usual when the local market is moving contrary to what the authorities want, the Bank of Korea checked banks' market positions. One dealer felt the central banks' phone calls were intended to keep banks from shorting the dollar Monday, but another said the checks weren't intended to influence market positions. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the greenback closed at NT33.513, from NT33.580 Friday, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars intermittently. \par \par \uc2  But the Indonesian rupiah bucked the regional trend and weakened, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand from importers surged, likely against any rising political uncertainty ahead of the annual session of the country's highest legislative body next month, and after weekend violence in troubled Aceh province. \par \par \uc2  State oil company Pertamina was suspected of buying U.S. dollars earlier in the day to finance the import of fuels. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Monday, the dollar was quoted at IDR8,900, from IDR8,850 late\par Friday.\par    -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254;\par nirmala.menon@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020708dy78000oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
