\par\par \b \uc2  China seen expanding  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  swap network by end-2002. \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2002\par 20:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, July 6 (Reuters) - China plans to sign bilateral swap agreements with the Philippines and Malaysia by the end of this year, extending an Asian-wide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety net designed to prevent future financial crisis, a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official said on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  These deals are part of the Chiang Mai Initiative (CMI), which aims to forge closer ties by creating a network of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps among the 10-strong Association of South East Asian Nations plus Japan, China and South Korea (ASEAN+3). \par \par \uc2  The swap pacts make \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves available at short notice to a country which faces a rapid deterioration in its balance of payments and whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under speculative attack, like the Thai baht in 1997. \par \par \uc2  "China has started its negotiation with the Philippines two to three weeks ago. China is also about to begin its negotiation with Malaysia," the official told Reuters on the sidelines of the Asia-Europe meeting (ASEM) in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  "I think China wants to seal deals with Malaysia and the Philippines by the end of this year. Korea is also very close to signing deals with Malaysia." \par \par \uc2  Japan, a driving force behind the CMI, already has bilateral swap agreements with South Korea ($2 billion), Thailand ($3 billion), the Philippines ($3 billion) and Malaysia ($1 billion). \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government is also in negotiations with Indonesia and Singapore about swap agreements. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a final arrangement with Indonesia and Singapore and we plan to sign the deal as soon as possible," the official said. \par \par \uc2  "The size of the deal with Indonesia would be similar to those we have signed already, which were worth around $3 billion although it might change in the future." \par \par \uc2  China and South Korea signed a $2 billion agreement in June. \par \par \uc2  The official said Korea has recently signed a deal with Thailand worth $1 billion. \par \par \uc2  Japan has a yen-yuan swap agreement with China worth $3 billion, signed in March, although the deal is seen as primarily symbolic because neither Japan nor China are likely to face liquidity or balance of payments in the short term. \par \par \uc2  Japan and China are the world's two biggest holders of external reserves. Japan's foreign reserves, the world's biggest, stood at $446.198 billion at end-June. China has more than $200 billion in foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  Japan has only one other yen-denominated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap line, with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that facility is mainly for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purposes and was used in September 2000 when the ECB and the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the euro. \par \par \uc2  ASEAN groups Brunei, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020706dy7600ngl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
