\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Higher On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk \b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2002\par 11:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended higher against the dollar Friday amid talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin pre-weekend trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at IDR8,845, lower than IDR8,860 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have sold around $20 million to help push the rupiah higher, traders said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  but never comments on its specific actions. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Friday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to an intraday low at IDR8,830 amid the reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At these low levels state banks bought the dollar on behalf of clients that need dollars to repay foreign debt and fund imports, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose Friday despite falls in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region against the dollar. The local economy is cut off from movements in the yen, the regional trendsetter. \par \par \uc2  Next week, the dollar is seen trading in a range between IDR8,750 and IDR8,900, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts remain bullish toward the rupiah in the medium term due to the government's plans to sell IDR150 trillion in non-performing loans by mid August. Money is expected to come in to Indonesia from Singapore and other financial centers to buy the loans and boost the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But others point to continued problems with the country's legal system, which is viewed as the most corrupt in the region, as deterring foreign investors. Foreign direct investment fell 59% in the first five months of this year compared with the same period of 2001. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian companies are also likely to repay about $5 billion in the last three quarters of 2002 to overseas creditors, again putting pressure on the local unit, the rupiah's detractors say. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Wright, Dow Jones Newswires; 6221 3983 1277; tom.wright@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020705dy75000hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
