\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Dlr Buoyed By Gain Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 11:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar was steady to firmer against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, drawing some support from its gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen's slippage was partly due to capital outflows from Japan associated with purchases of foreign securities by Japanese investors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which fell to a six-month low against the yen late last month, stabilized over the past week following a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities to cap the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  That stability has provided some assurance to Japanese investors looking to move their money offshore, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded as high as 128.60 yen during Asian hours before giving up some of its gains later in the session. Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at Y125.50, up from Y125.23 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was the main casualty of the weaker yen, falling against the dollar for the fourth time in five sessions. The Thai baht also retreated, while the New Taiwan dollar and Singapore dollar were little changed. The Indonesian rupiah strengthened after the government promised the International Monetary Fund that it would get tough with former bank owners that are refusing to pay billions of dollars they owe the state. \par \par \uc2  The won's slippage was welcome news for South Korean monetary authorities, who have repeatedly expressed concerns about the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength on the country's export competitiveness. The won hit an 18-month high against the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,233.6 won Wednesday, up from KRW1,225.5 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The state-run Korea Development Bank took advantage of the dollar's momentum, buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the afternoon to push it even higher, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  South Korean monetary authorities are most concerned about the won's competitiveness against the yen because of the overlap in exports between the two countries, said Andy Xie, an economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, Korea has an exchange rate based on its value against the Japanese yen. It is an effective policy to manage export growth and balance of payments for Korea," Xie said. \par \par \uc2  Expectations that the IMF would soon disburse more funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second day in a row, underpinned the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 8,700 rupiah, down from IDR8,765, late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they suspected Bank Indonesia sold up to $5 million via state banks to bolster the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after selling an estimated $3 million to $5 million Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the government said it has signed a new letter of intent with the IMF outlining economic reforms it plans to undertake as part of its loan program with the multilateral lender. \par \par \uc2  The IMF is expected to decide by the end of June on the disbursement of the next $340 million loan installment of a $5 billion program. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped offset U.S. dollar selling by interbank dealers. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.116, compared with NT$34.117 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to an intraday high of NT$34.175 in early trading following its strong performance overnight against the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, interbank dealers, who generally hold the view that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued, sold the U.S. dollar in the afternoon and pushed it to a low of NT$34.104. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then entered the market and bought the U.S. dollar to lift it before the close. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar recovered from a slow start to finish virtually unchanged against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7875, compared with S$1.7873 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar traded as high as $1.7945 in the first hours of trading on spill-over strength related to its gains against the yen before losing steam later in the session. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened in line with the yen, but equity-related inflows helped limit the baht's losses. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at 42.310 baht, up from its 16-month low of THB42.285 at the Asian close Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The peso wasn't traded because markets in Manila were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020612dy6c000jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
